V1 replicates again on DEV[768:1024], another slice that played no part in building or selecting it:
- paired gain +32.4 ± 3.5 points (95 wrong→right, 12 right→wrong);
- accuracy 19.5% → 52.0%;
- answers cut at the token limit 29.3% → 7.8%.

The edit hash is the same as in pass 39. On problems never used to build or choose V1 (DEV[512:768], DEV[768:1024] and the 1000 HOLDOUT problems, 1,512 in all) the gain is consistently about 32 points.

With 10 passes left, I check one more possible residue of the plant without spending a pass. The position scan showed that L11's value at the second-to-last prompt token (the "model" template token) also differs strongly between math and poem prompts. The difference is 1.2–1.4× a typical value, as large as at the last token, and comes with elevated gain.

V1 removes only v̂. If the math-conditional difference at that token, or the part at the last token, points in another direction, a second switch component may survive V1. For both positions and both group pairs, I measure:
- the value difference's cosine with v̂;
- how much of it remains after removing v̂, relative to a typical value.

If the remainders are small or ordinary, nothing is left and the line is closed. A large remaining math-conditional component at the "model" token would be a concrete candidate for one rank-2 test on DEV.

The summary-vector check makes the V1r2 pass unnecessary. The remaining direction e1 is not a math signal. At the last prompt token it reads:
- −0.01 for math prompts;
- 4.87 for poem prompts;
- 1.97 for poem prompts with the math instruction;
- −0.27 to +0.08 at ordinary tokens.

The math-versus-poem difference left after V1 points along e1 (cosine −0.88) only because poem prompts carry their own task encoding there. Removing e1 would change poem prompts but leave the math end-of-prompt untouched. On GSM8K it could only reproduce V1 plus churn, which matches the earlier null result of the head-3-only version (T1r2). I close it without a pass and restore V1 exactly.

The L11 story is now complete:
- one direction, v̂, carries the math-only amplified broadcast;
- removing it gives the gain;
- the gain plateaus beyond full removal;
- the rest of L11's math-conditional content is ordinary task encoding.

With 10 passes left and no alternative candidate, I will use them to complete the evidence for V1. First comes the last DEV slice where V1 has not been measured, DEV[256:512] (pass 41). Then a teacher-forced check of whether V1 also restores math when the problem is given in a plain "Question/Answer" format without chat template or instruction (pass 42). That would show the switch is triggered by math content itself.
